From 1e28c180e5c9d49b303ae86f0c95393027cc4e59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuubOS <nuubos@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 14:39:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Threaded rendering: draw each frame while the next one is
 emulated (nuubOS)

A Dreamcast game starts its render late in the frame (Dead or Alive 2:
after 18 of 18.5 ms of emulation), and the render was read (Process)
and drawn on the libretro thread right away while the emulation thread
waited, so the two threads took turns: ~31 ms a frame where each needed
~16.

Now the render the game starts in frame N is read and drawn at the start
of the retro_run() of frame N + 1, while the emulation thread runs that
frame, and handed to the frontend at once, so that the frontend's
presentation overlaps the emulation too. The frame shown is the one
before: one frame more of latency. Reading a render later is made safe
by taking everything it needs from the render itself: the registers
(ctx->regs; rend_pvr_regs is per thread now, and ta_vtx.cpp reads
through it) and what the region array says (ta_save_regions, at the
hand-over). Textures are still read from video memory, but a texture is
protected (or hashed) before it is decoded, so one the game rewrites
meanwhile is decoded again at its next use. A render to a texture is
still drawn at once with the emulation asleep, a frame taken from the
framebuffer still read at once.

Dead or Alive 2, 640x480, RG CubeXX, with the GLES changes: 30-36 ->
45-48 fps in a fight; Test Drive Le Mans 46-50 in a race.

Upstream: not submitted (nuubOS H700 tuning).
